WEBVTT

00:07.424 --> 00:09.509
Damn! What is this?

00:10.093 --> 00:12.470
The plane's engine turned pitch black!

00:15.348 --> 00:18.143
That's burnt sugar.

00:18.226 --> 00:20.186
They slipped it in right under our noses.

00:25.066 --> 00:27.402
We'll fix up the plane quickly,

00:28.278 --> 00:30.864
then go after those Science Club kids.

00:30.947 --> 00:32.741
We'll radio Brody.

00:32.824 --> 00:34.868
He can guide us
through the repairs remotely.

00:34.951 --> 00:37.454
Awesome, we landed a remote job!

00:38.038 --> 00:40.040
<i>In the middle of the Stone Age.</i>

00:46.713 --> 00:50.133
They're not even bothering
to encrypt their comms anymore?

00:50.216 --> 00:53.178
Even if we listen in,
there's no way we can counter it.

00:54.888 --> 00:56.222
Fine.

00:56.306 --> 00:59.976
Let's turn this into
a remote work battle too!

01:01.895 --> 01:06.524
<i>This is a key mission
for our Peace Zone team.</i>

01:07.150 --> 01:09.486
<i>Until we reach ground zero,</i>

01:10.070 --> 01:12.113
split the Medusa into parts

01:12.697 --> 01:15.075
and study them.

01:16.534 --> 01:18.078
ORIGINAL WORK: RIICHIRO INAGAKI, BOICHI
SHUEISHA "JUMP COMICS"

03:12.609 --> 03:15.862
<i>The river's current
is gentler than it looks.</i>

03:16.863 --> 03:17.947
<i>‎So...</i>

03:47.644 --> 03:51.314
We're aiming to revive
a million people for Corn City.

03:51.856 --> 03:54.192
Naturally, there'll be
some sick and injured ones.

03:54.776 --> 03:57.445
If we can just figure out
how this Medusa works,

03:57.528 --> 03:59.447
it might become our emergency doctor.

04:00.073 --> 04:03.201
Our very own Dr. Stone.

04:04.035 --> 04:06.371
<i>I hope you don't mind, Senku.</i>

04:06.454 --> 04:09.457
<i>If you're not going to explain yourself,</i>

04:09.540 --> 04:13.294
<i>then we'll just stick to what makes sense
and get it done.</i>

04:13.378 --> 04:15.380
<i>It makes sense.</i>

04:15.463 --> 04:18.466
<i>I've got no reason to oppose it, but...</i>

04:18.549 --> 04:22.178
<i>Isn't it a bit fishy
how suddenly it's happening now?</i>

04:22.262 --> 04:23.805
<i>Maybe I'm just overthinking it.</i>

04:25.098 --> 04:25.974
I see!

04:26.724 --> 04:30.979
Not even Nikki's group knows,
let alone the Americans,

04:31.062 --> 04:35.108
that we're heading toward
the source of the petrification beam!

04:35.191 --> 04:36.651
Exactly.

04:36.734 --> 04:38.695
We'll reach ground zero

04:38.778 --> 04:42.532
and get the original "Medusa"
that engulfed the Earth.

04:42.615 --> 04:45.827
Well, that's just wishful thinking.

04:45.910 --> 04:48.413
It could have run out of battery, too.

04:49.580 --> 04:52.292
But if we manage to take out
the fighter planes and machine guns

04:52.375 --> 04:56.921
and get rid of Stanley,
we'll be all down to Medusa.

04:57.505 --> 04:58.631
Yes.

04:58.715 --> 05:02.719
Even if we run out of battery,
we just have to keep moving.

05:03.428 --> 05:07.348
Got to pry open the actual device
with some remote work.

05:08.016 --> 05:12.895
Time for a full-on teardown
of the petrification device's mechanism.

05:17.859 --> 05:19.736
Whatever, I don't care.

05:19.819 --> 05:22.780
But we're the ones in charge
of handling the goods.

05:22.864 --> 05:25.616
The device stays under our surveillance.

05:26.492 --> 05:27.869
That's the condition.

05:29.203 --> 05:31.122
It'd be good to find out how it works.

05:31.748 --> 05:35.293
But I'm not so sure
if we can really dismantle it.

05:35.376 --> 05:39.297
Well, Stanley's gun did put a crack in it.

05:45.219 --> 05:46.512
Ouch...

05:47.096 --> 05:51.768
Even if we take Medusa apart,
we won't understand a thing to begin with.

05:51.851 --> 05:56.147
Shouldn't we leave this to technicians
with proper precision gear?

05:56.230 --> 05:57.607
Precision gear...

05:57.690 --> 06:02.028
Hey, don't you know
some good engineers, journalist?

06:02.111 --> 06:08.242
There are plenty of famous, high-quality
watch and jewelry makers in America, but...

06:08.326 --> 06:09.577
That's it! Let's go there!

06:09.660 --> 06:12.246
There's no way you remember
places like that!

06:15.083 --> 06:16.459
Luxury watches?

06:17.710 --> 06:18.628
Well...

06:21.881 --> 06:23.549
It's kind of far,

06:23.633 --> 06:26.219
but I think Rodex's headquarters
are near the airport.

06:26.803 --> 06:27.720
Everybody knows that.

06:27.804 --> 06:29.555
- No, we don't!
- No, we don't!

06:29.639 --> 06:31.724
If they release any watches,
I pretty much...

06:33.851 --> 06:34.727
want them all!

06:34.811 --> 06:35.812
Don't you?

06:35.895 --> 06:37.605
- No, we don't!
- No, we don't!

06:37.688 --> 06:41.109
I think you're the only one
who knows all about that, Ryusui.

06:41.692 --> 06:42.777
But in this world,

06:42.860 --> 06:44.821
I can't tell which direction it's in.

06:44.904 --> 06:47.240
I got this! Just give me the lowdown.

06:47.824 --> 06:50.118
Then I'll sketch out a map.

06:55.957 --> 07:00.128
It'd be great if we could send this map
to Yuzuriha and the folks in Corn City!

07:01.337 --> 07:02.380
"Send"?

07:03.172 --> 07:04.549
I see.

07:04.632 --> 07:06.926
Even after all this time,
we just can't shake it off.

07:07.009 --> 07:09.637
Our civilized 21st-century life...

07:10.680 --> 07:12.265
Well, then.

07:12.348 --> 07:14.142
For the sake of our remote job,

07:14.725 --> 07:16.060
shall I make a fax?

07:16.144 --> 07:17.478
- What?
- What?

07:17.562 --> 07:19.021
A fax?

07:19.105 --> 07:21.232
Something that lets you
send pictures remotely.

07:21.315 --> 07:22.775
But how...?

07:24.235 --> 07:25.862
The fax is ready.

07:25.945 --> 07:27.405
That was fast!

07:31.951 --> 07:33.077
A gridded...

07:34.162 --> 07:35.413
graph paper?

07:36.497 --> 07:38.166
Seriously?

07:40.751 --> 07:42.211
No way!

07:42.295 --> 07:44.505
So, he's sending one square at a time,

07:44.589 --> 07:47.633
using black-and-white Morse code,
starting from the top-left.

07:47.717 --> 07:48.885
It's a manual fax.

07:48.968 --> 07:50.511
Why me?

07:50.595 --> 07:53.389
Why pick me for such tedious, awful tasks?

07:53.473 --> 07:56.267
Osamu Tezuka used a similar method

07:56.350 --> 07:59.395
to create manga remotely
while he was overseas.

07:59.479 --> 08:02.732
So, it can really be done...

08:36.557 --> 08:38.809
(MANUAL) FAX ACQUIRED!

08:47.568 --> 08:51.614
<i>I interviewed Rodex's CEO once
before turning into stone.</i>

08:52.448 --> 08:54.075
<i>I'd remember her face.</i>

08:55.535 --> 08:57.537
<i>It's her, no doubt.</i>

09:00.039 --> 09:01.332
So...

09:03.251 --> 09:06.671
In that case,
I've got just the person for the job.

09:07.421 --> 09:09.382
Our ace...

09:27.316 --> 09:31.237
Argh! What happened to me?
Where're my clothes? Where am I?

09:31.320 --> 09:33.823
- Honest reactions are refreshing.
- What is going on...?

09:33.906 --> 09:37.159
Yes, the people we woke up until now
all had insane mental strength.

09:45.084 --> 09:48.296
I know you just woke up, but...

09:49.213 --> 09:51.382
Nikki is explaining something.

09:51.465 --> 09:53.593
Are you listening, kid?

09:53.676 --> 09:54.802
Did you call me "kid"?

09:55.595 --> 09:58.514
That's not how you talk to someone
you're asking a favor from.

09:59.056 --> 10:01.434
My name is Joel.

10:01.517 --> 10:04.312
Is it too hard for you to memorize?

10:04.395 --> 10:07.440
Okay, gotcha. Didn't mean to be rude.

10:07.523 --> 10:10.109
Could you open up
the petrification device to examine it?

10:10.192 --> 10:13.195
We're counting on your skills, Joel.

10:13.279 --> 10:14.572
All right?

10:15.156 --> 10:18.409
This is the device.
It's a bit cracked, though...

10:21.621 --> 10:23.205
<i>It's not that he's not listening...</i>

10:23.289 --> 10:26.751
<i>He's so focused on work
that he's clueless when it comes to women.</i>

10:28.711 --> 10:29.545
On your arm...

10:30.379 --> 10:31.547
Is that a watch?

10:32.131 --> 10:34.133
I'm amazed! Did you craft it?

10:34.216 --> 10:35.092
Is it handmade?

10:35.176 --> 10:36.302
Yes...

10:36.385 --> 10:39.680
Awesome! You just got here,
and you did it yourself already?

10:39.764 --> 10:43.851
I used some of your tools and utensils
without asking.

10:43.934 --> 10:48.689
A wristwatch is a mark of a man
who values punctuality.

10:48.773 --> 10:51.692
The precision sucks,
but it beats going bare-handed.

10:51.776 --> 10:53.986
You act way too tough around guys!

10:54.070 --> 10:55.696
Let me see.

10:55.780 --> 10:58.115
What's with this device...

10:59.700 --> 11:01.994
This...

11:02.078 --> 11:06.332
This isn't like any science
we were familiar with in the 21st century.

11:06.415 --> 11:07.583
Yes...

11:11.128 --> 11:12.838
Let me focus...

11:13.714 --> 11:15.716
Feel free to watch.

11:15.800 --> 11:19.804
But if anyone talks,
I won't hesitate to kill them.

11:25.935 --> 11:27.812
Matsukaze said

11:27.895 --> 11:30.815
everything left of the Medusa
on the island was destroyed.

11:30.898 --> 11:32.525
Wait a sec!

11:32.608 --> 11:35.569
So you're saying
even some random tool could smash it?

11:35.653 --> 11:39.073
Taking it apart carefully
is another matter, though.

11:46.831 --> 11:48.457
<i>Most of the parts</i>

11:48.541 --> 11:51.085
<i>look like they were made
with a 3D printer.</i>

11:51.919 --> 11:55.506
<i>And the whole circuit is made of glass?</i>

11:55.589 --> 11:57.383
<i>Or maybe some mineral?</i>

11:58.175 --> 12:01.345
<i>I don't think I can
fully understand how it works.</i>

12:01.929 --> 12:04.140
<i>But just like a wristwatch,</i>

12:04.223 --> 12:07.393
<i>I'm sure every part has a role to play.</i>

12:10.563 --> 12:11.814
<i>What is this?</i>

12:12.857 --> 12:14.442
<i>It's blackened, but...</i>

12:16.944 --> 12:21.031
The core parts connected all around
are diamonds?

12:21.866 --> 12:25.494
<i>If it's just a worn-out battery,</i>

12:25.578 --> 12:27.746
<i>maybe we could take it out,
fix it, and put it back</i>

12:29.123 --> 12:30.624
<i>for once...</i>

12:40.384 --> 12:42.887
If the petrification device
were reactivated,

12:42.970 --> 12:47.683
it could be controlled by voice command
for activation timing and range.

12:48.309 --> 12:50.978
This isn't something you should try alone.

12:54.648 --> 12:56.108
One meter...

12:56.942 --> 12:58.611
One second...

13:17.838 --> 13:19.423
Messing with the internal connections

13:19.507 --> 13:22.885
was a last-ditch effort
to get it working, right?

13:22.968 --> 13:23.886
Maybe...

13:23.969 --> 13:25.554
It's not shining anymore.

13:25.638 --> 13:27.723
It's useless for practical use.

13:27.806 --> 13:28.974
Wait a minute!

13:29.058 --> 13:33.229
Has giving up become a trend
in this Stone World?

13:33.812 --> 13:35.689
This is a job for a professional.

13:35.773 --> 13:37.858
Let me investigate thoroughly.

13:37.942 --> 13:40.236
Two seconds ago,
you were all nervous around the girls,

13:40.319 --> 13:42.696
but now you're acting tough with us guys!

13:43.572 --> 13:47.910
No matter how tough it gets,
I'll make it through

13:47.993 --> 13:50.454
<i>while mountains still stand.</i>

13:52.331 --> 13:55.000
Boom, let's blaze over these mountains!

13:56.377 --> 13:58.212
This is our turning point!

13:58.295 --> 14:01.715
"Turning point"? But this is the Andes.

14:01.799 --> 14:04.677
The punchline relies on it
being a Japanese idiom.

14:04.760 --> 14:06.303
The Andes!

14:06.387 --> 14:09.265
I feel like I've heard about this before!

14:09.348 --> 14:10.307
It's crazy.

14:10.391 --> 14:13.435
The mountains go on and on
without a break.

14:14.311 --> 14:16.897
We definitely can't go around them.

14:16.981 --> 14:20.651
No matter how you look at it,
we'll have to get across them.

14:29.618 --> 14:31.996
As for the mechanics
of the petrification device,

14:32.079 --> 14:34.915
we'll let Corn City take care of it
through remote analysis

14:34.999 --> 14:39.128
<i>as we head straight to the epicenter
of humanity's petrification</i>

14:39.211 --> 14:40.379
<i>to find the real thing.</i>

14:41.255 --> 14:43.549
Finding and activating
the petrification weapon

14:43.632 --> 14:47.094
is the key to beating Stanley.

14:47.177 --> 14:51.557
It's a long journey,
so please make sure to enjoy this meal.

14:51.640 --> 14:55.019
I barely have any time left.

14:55.102 --> 14:58.898
Stanley's probably just about
finished repairing his plane.

15:00.774 --> 15:03.402
Reaching the Amazon rainforest
means victory,

15:03.485 --> 15:08.032
<i>thanks to the ridiculously
huge trees creating a natural barrier.</i>

15:08.115 --> 15:11.452
<i>No airplane will be able
to bomb or land there.</i>

15:11.535 --> 15:13.203
<i>I see!</i>

15:13.287 --> 15:16.707
The forest barrier will be all around,
wherever we go.

15:16.790 --> 15:18.250
We can't really know.

15:18.334 --> 15:20.836
Nature has changed a lot
in the past thousands of years,

15:20.920 --> 15:23.130
just like what we've seen in Japan.

15:26.383 --> 15:29.511
Hang in there! This is our turning point.

15:29.595 --> 15:31.055
Once we cross the Andes,

15:31.138 --> 15:34.433
it'll be nothing but dense, dense forest!

15:35.017 --> 15:36.685
I don't get it.

15:36.769 --> 15:40.272
Shouldn't this place have turned
into a desert after all these years?

15:41.231 --> 15:43.692
Can the geographer see the terrain?

15:44.568 --> 15:45.736
Yes, somehow.

15:45.819 --> 15:47.154
I can make a guess.

15:47.237 --> 15:48.906
The snow in Japan, for example.

15:48.989 --> 15:50.783
Does it snow more than in the old world?

15:50.866 --> 15:53.160
- Huh?!
- Yes, that's true.

15:53.243 --> 15:54.078
That's why...

15:54.662 --> 15:59.625
<i>Senku and the rest of us were unable
to fight until the winter thawed.</i>

15:59.708 --> 16:02.544
How could you know that
if you haven't been there?

16:02.628 --> 16:04.004
It's halfway across the globe!

16:04.088 --> 16:05.005
Easy peasy!

16:05.714 --> 16:09.760
<i>After humanity was petrified,
global warming subsided,</i>

16:09.843 --> 16:14.348
<i>and cold currents from the south
reached all the way north.</i>

16:14.431 --> 16:17.434
It's true that the seawater
near the Equator was cold.

16:18.435 --> 16:20.229
- The La Niña phenomenon!
- That's right!

16:20.312 --> 16:22.690
But I don't get it at all.

16:22.773 --> 16:23.941
Tell me more.

16:24.024 --> 16:25.693
<i>When the ocean cools down,</i>

16:25.776 --> 16:28.737
<i>the heat moves this way and stirs the air.</i>

16:28.821 --> 16:32.741
<i>Once stirred,
the cold northern air moves in.</i>

16:32.825 --> 16:34.785
It's cold! It's going to snow a lot!

16:36.078 --> 16:40.040
Simply put,
when seawater cools near South America,

16:40.124 --> 16:42.543
it leads to snowfall
halfway around the globe in Japan.

16:43.585 --> 16:45.212
I get it now!

16:46.255 --> 16:47.381
It's crazy...

16:48.507 --> 16:52.177
It's like the whole Earth
is connected all around...

16:53.512 --> 16:54.847
through science!

16:56.432 --> 16:58.225
When the seawater gets cold,

16:58.308 --> 17:00.978
we get less evaporation,
which means no rain.

17:02.271 --> 17:04.732
So that's what's causing
the desertification around here.

17:04.815 --> 17:07.985
But behind the mountain wall,

17:08.068 --> 17:10.237
the sea doesn't matter one bit.

17:10.821 --> 17:12.948
<i>So it's a different world
across the mountains,</i>

17:13.032 --> 17:15.743
<i>and the Amazon rainforest
spreads far and wide.</i>

17:15.826 --> 17:17.077
Which means...

17:17.661 --> 17:19.121
It's time to switch seats

17:19.204 --> 17:21.206
to blaze through
this freakish mountain route!

17:21.290 --> 17:22.583
Switch seats?

17:22.666 --> 17:24.918
For example, how much do you weigh, Luna?

17:26.754 --> 17:28.630
Come on, Chelsea!

17:28.714 --> 17:32.426
What do you mean?
Why can't she ask about her weight?

17:36.388 --> 17:38.182
Around 90 pounds.

17:38.265 --> 17:39.433
That's our Lady!

17:39.516 --> 17:40.434
Your style, too...

17:40.517 --> 17:42.519
Wait, you'd be too skinny!

17:42.603 --> 17:45.564
Come on, tell us the real numbers...

17:45.647 --> 17:46.565
Yes.

17:46.648 --> 17:49.443
We're going to use our weights
to balance our bikes.

17:49.526 --> 17:53.030
I see. We ended up being distributed
randomly because we were in a hurry.

17:53.113 --> 17:54.948
We're pushing hard
trying to climb off-road

17:55.032 --> 17:56.909
with three people per bike.

17:56.992 --> 18:00.704
We need to balance our weights,
or the heavier groups won't make it up.

18:00.788 --> 18:03.123
Bike group assignments...

18:03.207 --> 18:04.792
Let's fly, Luna.

18:04.875 --> 18:06.543
To the other side of the night.

18:06.627 --> 18:08.921
May I ask...? I mean...

18:09.004 --> 18:10.798
The numbers on the scale are important,

18:10.881 --> 18:12.382
so I'd like to know

18:12.466 --> 18:16.970
if Senku and I riding together
would be perfectly balanced...

18:17.638 --> 18:21.350
I see now! You're falling for Senku!

18:21.433 --> 18:22.726
Romance talk, I love it!

18:22.810 --> 18:24.186
<i>Come on, Chelsea!</i>

18:27.898 --> 18:30.484
It's nothing worth all the fuss, though...

18:30.567 --> 18:32.319
We're already "dating."

18:32.402 --> 18:35.197
You know, that cool,
"grown-up" kind of relationship?

18:35.280 --> 18:36.657
What?

18:36.740 --> 18:38.826
Is that actually true?

18:39.535 --> 18:40.494
Yes.

18:40.577 --> 18:43.831
When we talked at the Perseus's infirmary...

18:47.459 --> 18:49.128
If you recover...

18:49.837 --> 18:51.421
please, be my boyfriend.

18:55.968 --> 18:58.220
Like a marriage of convenience?

18:59.513 --> 19:03.892
Come to think of it,
you've got your own crew, right?

19:05.269 --> 19:07.312
Well...

19:07.396 --> 19:11.942
Actually, as a mighty woman, I said that
assuming you'd be prepared for it.

19:13.485 --> 19:15.821
I don't understand those kinds
of power dynamics.

19:16.405 --> 19:20.117
I'm just thankful to have
gained a couple more allies.

19:20.826 --> 19:23.704
You really are a capable one, Luna.

19:24.204 --> 19:25.998
Squee...

19:30.127 --> 19:32.212
Maybe you're not actually dating...

19:32.296 --> 19:33.589
<i>Come on, Chelsea.</i>

19:33.672 --> 19:34.798
Really?

19:36.133 --> 19:37.801
It's Senku, after all.

19:37.885 --> 19:40.554
I thought that was probably the case.

19:41.388 --> 19:43.015
I need to try harder...

19:43.098 --> 19:44.725
I see.

19:45.309 --> 19:49.563
Maybe Chelsea's trying to break the ice
with this sudden love talk

19:49.646 --> 19:52.816
so everyone feels comfortable
sharing their weight...

19:52.900 --> 19:56.445
No, not at all. I just felt like asking.

19:56.528 --> 19:59.406
It's pointless to count weights
based on what you remember.

19:59.948 --> 20:02.451
You've been living
in this Stone World for years.

20:02.534 --> 20:05.495
I'm ten billion percent sure
they have changed.

20:06.788 --> 20:08.373
- There you go.
- Huh?

20:09.416 --> 20:10.751
What is that?

20:10.834 --> 20:13.337
<i>Right after my resurrection,</i>

20:13.420 --> 20:16.465
<i>I got myself a ruler
based on my height of 171 cm.</i>

20:16.548 --> 20:20.052
<i>Then, if we have a 10x10x10 cm cube,</i>

20:20.135 --> 20:22.679
<i>the water it holds weighs exactly 1 kg.</i>

20:24.973 --> 20:25.849
Whoa!

20:25.933 --> 20:27.935
SCALE ACQUIRED!

20:51.083 --> 20:53.085
Let's go!

21:08.183 --> 21:10.435
I guess this is as far as we can get.

21:11.103 --> 21:13.563
We can't afford to stop and worry.

21:14.356 --> 21:17.818
Stanley's crew will be arriving soon
on their repaired plane.

21:17.901 --> 21:22.239
We'll have to ditch our bikes and luggage
and climb with our own bare hands.

21:22.322 --> 21:24.241
What? No way!

21:24.324 --> 21:26.618
Even if we could, we'd die right after!

21:26.702 --> 21:29.162
No, we'll carry everything with us.

21:35.252 --> 21:38.588
All right. Let's take off.

21:53.478 --> 21:55.605
We'll deal with that airplane

21:56.189 --> 21:58.942
using scientific 3D maneuver gear!

22:03.739 --> 22:05.115
<i>A ropeway!</i>

22:05.198 --> 22:08.994
<i>This is the way
we're taking the Andes in one go!</i>

22:09.077 --> 22:10.912
<i>Oh-ho!</i>

22:10.996 --> 22:13.290
You're starting to build it now?

22:13.373 --> 22:15.208
In a place like this?

23:53.014 --> 23:55.892
